  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates monosemanticity of learned SAE features in a vision-language model, not the claim's neuron poles, neuron-explanation predictive accuracy, or attribution-graph units. Its results concern a related but different object.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports measurements of SAE-feature monosemanticity and semantic alignment, recorded below as related findings. It does not test the stated shares of neuron poles, explanation prediction on held-out activations, or neuron-based attribution graphs. Results about the SAE summarization pipeline's identification rate and reconstruction/sparsity metrics do not measure the claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study trains structured sparse autoencoders on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct residual streams and scores learned SAE features (MS score, mIoU, consistency rate). It does not audit the model's own neurons or neuron poles. It does not score neuron explanations by simulating activations, test InceptionV1 neurons, or build neuron-based attribution graphs. Its 'neurons' are SAE latents, and its results compare the authors' own method against a vanilla SAE. Its results therefore bear on no version of the claim.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that the structured SAE improves visual semantic alignment and sparsity efficiency while keeping explained variance above 0.99. It also improves language-side monosemanticity and cross-modal consistency of multimodal SAE features. They further conclude that their hierarchical interpretation pipeline is more reliable than direct VLM summarization.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains structured sparse autoencoders with visual-region group and exclusive sparsity on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct, then evaluates SAE features; it does not audit neuron poles or test neuron explanations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary by layer and feature subgroup: multimodal-feature MS scores improve at all four layers, while vision-only MS scores decline at layers 10, 15, and 20 and language-only MS declines at layer 5. The clustering analysis selects alpha = 0.02, with the stated balance range alpha ∈ [0.01, 0.02].",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Structured SAE regularization raised MS scores for multimodal SAE features at all four layers. Results were mixed for vision-only and language-only subgroups. Cross-modal consistency rose at all layers.",
        "proposed_claim": "Structured visual regularization improves measured monosemanticity of multimodal SAE features in a VLM, but not uniformly across feature subgroups.",
